builds, no-oxigraph check, wasm dependency gate, pure-core source gate,
tests) after one cargo fmt pass to fix formatting. doctor shows the same
8 pre-existing charter-document-without-id warnings as before the change;
no new warnings or violations.$ !3 ^2026-09-24T11:22 %2026-09-25T20:44 #01a0d4a7-f0cc-74ef-86bb-27e5d766535f
[x] Scope index views to one charter =charter-scoped-queue $DECIDED 2026-09-25: option c plus stamping. A charter with no id: in its frontmatter loads with a fresh random UUID every load, and rdf::anonymize_charters strips that ephemeral id from the published dataset, so no query can filter on it; a sandbox run found this on 2026-09-25 after resolving via one load and filtering a second load silently matched nothing. Step 1: stamp a durable id on every charter in every platform workspace with clearhead normalize file CHARTER.md --write, one commit per repo; doctor's declares-no-id warnings list them and must be gone afterwards. Step 2: add --charter to index views, matching only charters with a stamped id, by alias, UUID or name, including child charters; a charter without an id is a clear error that names normalize as the fix. Rejected: matching alias or title text inside each SPARQL view, which loses ambiguity detection; threading an ephemeral charter mapping from load to query, which is plumbing for identity that should simply be durable. Why: the agent loop in scripts/agent-run needs one charter's queue to hand an agent a larger chunk of work. DONE 2026-09-25: Step 1 -- normalize --write stamped ids on all 8 no-id platform charters (agent-surface, someday and its 6 children, commit a55366d) and the clearhead-cli root charter (clearhead-core commit 3577658); doctor is clean workspace-wide, no declares-no-id warnings remain anywhere. Step 2 -- clearhead query index \[name] --charter <alias|uuid|name> added (clearhead-core commit bb0667e, platform submodule bump 3a43484): resolves via the existing resolve_charter_across_workspaces/select_reference lookup (alias, UUID, or name), requires CharterIdSource::Document (errors naming normalize as the fix otherwise), and scopes rows via a hasSubCharter* semi-join against each view's ?owner binding (added to default/weekly/chain, already present in unscheduled), so child charters are included. Review: NO OTHER-VENDOR REVIEWER WAS AVAILABLE in this sandbox -- used a fresh same-vendor (Claude) general-purpose subagent instead, noting the runbook's vendor-diversity rationale is not satisfied here. Its one finding (no test coverage for --charter) was fixed by adding two tests to query_index.rs (scoping + sub-charter inclusion; no-id error path), both passing. Gate green (fmt, clippy, both no-default-features builds, oxigraph/wasm/pure-core gates, full test suite).$ !3 ^2026-09-25T14:27 %2026-09-25T22:34 #01a0da77-920d-79b3-996a-9a7b77073f13
[x] Conform to the spec\: charters without a declared state are New =charters-default-new $Spec changed 2026-09-25, specifications 86e4137, charters.md and workspace.md: a charter with no document, the root without charters/README.md included, is New like every new charter; New is the planning state and activation is always an explicit transition. Core disagrees in three observed places. One: loading treats a charter with no document as Active, root and non-root; it must be New, so its actions leave engagement queries until activated. Two: clearhead init writes the root README with state Active, seen 2026-09-25 in ontology and specifications; it must write New and print the activation command, clearhead update charter NAME --state active. Three: jot created clearhead-core's root README with a path-derived UUIDv5 id, af439c6c-83af-5ba4-aaa9-e15a76ee2230, against the spec rule that root identity is minted once and never derived from a path; jot never sets or changes state, and must not mint a path-derived id. Required with it, since hidden-until-activated becomes the normal case: add action into a New charter prints that the action is hidden and the activation command, and doctor warns about every New charter that owns open actions; these overlap materialize-implicit-charters, do them once. Existing roots all declare Active, so current workspaces keep their queues; check any charter still without a document with doctor before landing.  Document the behavior where the CLI's users will look: crates/clearhead-cli/README.md, beside Charter identity normalization, covering New as the planning state, the activation command, and what init, jot, normalize and doctor do about state.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T17:02 %2026-09-26T00:40 #01a0db04-f5e0-7bb2-8389-7433aeb83f1c
[x] Make debug and query list emit JSON when piped =debug-list-json $DECIDED 2026-09-29: the CLI's output rule is TTY detection, human at a terminal and machine output when piped (orient.rs run, index.rs default_index_format, verb errors in cli/mod.rs). Two read commands break it and always print human text: debug (cli/debug.rs, println throughout) and query list (query/sparql/registry.rs list, comfy_table only). Fix both the way orient does it: build one serde-serializable struct, then print_report at a TTY or serde_json when piped. No new --format flag. debug JSON carries config (file paths with found/active flags, data_dir, config_dir, default_file, plan_component, additional_workspaces) and workspace (resolved_data_root, resolution as cwd-walk, env, config or xdg-default, workspace_id, workspace_name, root_charter, charters with alias, file, parent, source, and the graph_summary counts). query list JSON is an array of name, type, source, with type null for the flat namespace (the table's dash). Human output stays byte-identical, so pin it with a test before refactoring. Consumer: clearhead.nvim, which today parses the box table (query.parse_list) and guesses the workspace for its quickfix titles; its nvim-subcommands action list-json switches over once this lands.$ !3 ^2026-09-29T23:11 %2026-09-30T06:47 #01a0f0f0-6021-7611-aa6c-be9d5d71ccd0
[x] Fix review findings on debug and query list JSON =debug-json-review-fixes $DECIDED 2026-09-29, review of landed run 20260929-233844 (5d8202b), verdict land after fixes. Human output was verified byte-identical against the old binary at a TTY, so keep both pinning tests green and unchanged. 1) debug JSON must not carry display placeholders: parent and root_charter become null instead of a dash, data_dir and config_dir become null when unset instead of the angle-bracket labels; the human renderer maps null back to the same text. query list already does this for its type. 2) Remove the citations of a specifications/workspace.md TTY output rule in debug.rs and the registry.rs test comment: no such rule exists in the spec. Cite orient.rs and index.rs default_index_format as the precedent instead. 3) debug must degrade: before the refactor it printed the config section before reading the workspace, so a broken workspace still showed config. Build the config section first and make a workspace read failure a reported error field in the JSON and an error line after the config section in the human output, as orient degrades rather than failing. 4) Collapse FileStatus and ProjectFileStatus into one struct (path plus exists) and replace the two parallel Options with one Option holding both project files, removing the expect in print_report. 5) In registry.rs, carry the family as an Option through list_rows and render None as the dash in the table, instead of comparing against the dash to recover None.$ !3 ^2026-09-29T23:56 %2026-09-30T06:59 #01a0f119-a7b9-7420-92f7-e2271be027cb
