[x] Materialize implicit charters when they need an id =materialize-implicit-charters $DECIDED 2026-09-25: option a. A charter with no .md file is implicit, synthesized from its .actions files; clearhead-core, cli-composability, lsp, ontology and specifications are all implicit. It has no durable id, so query index --charter rejects it, and the error tells the user to run normalize on a charter file that does not exist. A sandbox run on 2026-09-25 found this after stamping every explicit charter; doctor stayed clean because it only warns about explicit charters. Decision: anything referenced must exist, so asking for a durable id on an implicit charter creates its .md with the same defaults as clearhead add charter: fresh id, alias and title from the stem, state New. Reuse that scaffold rather than writing a second one. Surfaces: the stamping path materializes the file; the --charter no-id error names a command that works for implicit charters; doctor warns about implicit charters as it does about undeclared ids. Then materialize the five above, one commit per repo. Rejected, option b, a UUIDv5 derived from workspace and stem: identity would move whenever a path is renamed.  REVISED 2026-09-25 by the human, correcting the state wording above: new charters start New, and that is right; Active is a deliberate state transition, and it is what puts a charter's actions into queries. So materializing an implicit charter correctly writes state New, which hides its open actions until someone activates it. What was missing is the reminder, which must be loud: when materialization writes the file, the CLI output names the open actions now hidden and the exact activation command, clearhead update charter NAME --state active; doctor warns about any New charter with open actions. jot must write state New explicitly rather than omit it: on 2026-09-25 a jot created this repo's root charter with no state, which hid all 8 of its open actions from the queue until it was activated by hand.  REFINED 2026-09-25 by the human: jot never sets or changes state; if the charter it writes has none, so be it. init, add charter and normalize's materialization write state explicitly. Because an absent state reads as New, a jot-created file still hides its charter's open actions, so the doctor warning about a New charter with open actions is the catch-all that covers every path, jot included; the CLI message when a file is created is the extra for the verbs that create charters.  REVIEW 2026-09-25, pi with gpt-5.6-sol over the landed run, verdict land after fixes, nothing blocking; all three are for the next session on this action. Should-fix, store/assembly.rs line 353: doctor prints a normalize path relative to the charter directory, e.g. work.md instead of .clearhead/charters/work.md, so the printed command fails from the project root; Core lacks the root, so the CLI adapter should qualify it. Should-fix, cli/file.rs line 213: the materializer duplicates add_charter's scaffold from cli/charter.rs lines 419 to 430, against this action's explicit decision to reuse it; extract one shared scaffold taking id, title, alias and parent. Nit, cli/charter.rs line 33: charter_md_path re-derives what the new document_anchor_for_actions in store/pathing.rs provides; use it. EXECUTED 2026-09-30: addressed the three remaining review items in clearhead-core commit 26a2edc. The native doctor adapter qualifies the Core document placeholder using the workspace charter root, for both implicit and existing documents; regression coverage executes its suggested normalization command from the project root for named and nested primary charters. add charter and normalize materialization now share new_charter(id, title, alias, parent), preserving explicit New state. charter_md_path delegates to document_anchor_for_actions. The previously landed materialization and charter files were already present; no additional repository materialization was needed. Full scripts/gate.sh passed; doctor before and after reported the same three existing New-charter/open-action warnings and no violations. No new dependencies.$ !2 ^2026-09-25T15:37 %2026-09-30T21:56 #01a0dab7-5abc-7b52-94d6-5c30171b9a13
[x] Treat a missing configured workspace the same way in every verb $DECIDED 2026-10-08 with the human: specifications f16ee56 (configuration.md, Error Handling; after v0.2.1, so not in the pinned v0.2.0): "Missing additional workspace: Skip an additional_workspaces entry that does not exist, in every verb, with a warning naming the entry; doctor reports it. Never create it: the entry is stale config, not a directory to initialize, and one stale entry must not break work in the others." Implement that in Core for every verb, and cover delete action in a test. Found 2026-09-30: platform's config.json still listed ../clearhead-core/crates/clearhead-lsp after the crate merge removed that directory. read, add and update kept working, but delete action failed with 'Failed to list workspace ... Invalid path'. Pick one behavior for a configured workspace path that does not exist (a doctor warning plus skipping it seems right: the config is the user's, and one stale entry should not break a write elsewhere) and apply it in every verb; cover delete in a test. IMPLEMENTED 2026-10-08 in clearhead-core commit fbbc877: CommandContext resolves additional_workspaces once per invocation, warns on stderr naming each missing entry and resolved path, excludes it from workspace fan-out and the shared WorkspaceConfig, and retains structured findings for doctor. Doctor reports missing-additional-workspace warnings in text and JSON, including fix and dry-run, without creating or repairing stale entries. Integration tests cover show, update, complete, delete (including completed sibling actions), add, read, global-config debug, doctor JSON/fix/dry-run, a healthy additional workspace beside a stale entry, and non-creation of missing parent directories. Full Core gate passed; doctor after remains at the baseline 13 warnings and zero violations. No new dependencies.$ !3 ^2026-09-30T15:27 %2026-10-08T22:42 #01a0f46e-6397-73d3-85dc-bdf7d87747c3
